y  here in front of me that it is a rather  complicated procedure before we even  begin drawing there is a serious amount  of work that has to go in to  understanding what we're actually  designing for example a written design  document brief as you can see over here  is a critical element of the whole  design process we have to tease out what  you're looking to design before we know  what we're designing so that document is  ever growing ever evolving and  eventually becomes what you're looking  for now let's say we've presented you  with a fee proposal you've accepted it  it's all good we're going to move on to  sketch design once we move on to sketch  design the first thing we're going to do  is establish a feature survey of that  site that can come in a number of  different forms it can come from you  directly or it can come from a surveyor  that surveyor is going to provide us  some digital data that we can import  into our 3d software for me personally  that is archicad which allows us to  understand the contours and the levels  of the site the site is complex even if  it seems flat it's definitely not flat  so what we're looking at understanding  is the boundaries the power poles the  underground services and anything that  might be an issue for us later down the  track that feature survey is always done  before we even start drawing once we  have that feature survey information  then we begin actually drawing not many  architects still draw on paper but there  are still some out there that do a  phenomenal job so it doesn't matter what  your first sketch paper or digital it's  going to be something exquisite the  first sketch is always something that we  have no idea what we're presenting to  you we don't know if that's your brief  truly if that's your budget truly but  it's a starting point it's where we  actually understand  what you're looking at what you want and  how we can move forward in that project  together now after we establish that  sketch design have the meeting with  yourself and anybody else that's  involved in that project very early on  then we can move on to design  development design development is the  second part of this project where we  continue to draw yes there's a lot of  drawing and start but  after this design development it gets a  lot more political very similar to  sketch design the design development  phase is really working through that  architectural plan understanding all the  rooms the spaces the colors the grand  scheme of the architecture we don't get  into too much detail here we're not  picking any selections we're not picking  any fixtures or main colors internally  we're just focusing on the outside and  the position of our walls now let's say  all of that was quite simple we've  mastered it we've moved on it becomes  even more complicated if we're  discussing a residential project versus  a commercial project so residential  project for example would start engaging  three different consultants we'd  undertake a bowel assessment which is a  bushfire attack level if it's in a  bushfire zone an energy efficiency  consultant and also a structural  engineers quote if it's commercial on  the other hand well things get a little  bit more complicated let me just zoom  out a little bit here for you before we  break it down this is the commercial  strain that makes up all of these  consultants so once we start getting  into a complex commercial project that's  where an architect's  real understanding of project management  contract administration documentation  and the whole journey from start to  finish of that building and the life  cycle  is critical if the architect isn't  experienced enough to know what happens  when and when something is due then the  project can cause some sort of problem  some sort of delays so if we look back  into it once again we use those three  same consultants we started with  residential they're still required in  commercial especially our structural  en